You can achieve this by wrapping your `input` and SVG icon within a container (e.g., a `div`) and using CSS to position the icon. Here's a breakdown of how to do it with HTML, CSS, and some JavaScript:

**HTML (index.html):**

```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
    <meta charset="UTF-8">
    <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1.0">
    <title>Input with Search Icon</title>
    <link rel="stylesheet" href="style.css">
</head>
<body>

    <div class="search-container">
        <input type="text" id="searchInput" placeholder="Enter your search term...">
        <button type="button" class="search-icon-button" onclick="handleSearch()">
            <!-- SVG Reading Glass Icon (example) -->
            <svg class="search-icon" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg">
                <path d="M11 19C15.4183 19 19 15.4183 19 11C19 6.58172 15.4183 3 11 3C6.58172 3 3 6.58172 3 11C3 15.4183 6.58172 19 11 19Z" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"/>
                <path d="M21 21L16.65 16.65" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"/>
            </svg>
        </button>
    </div>

    <script src="script.js"></script>
</body>
</html>
```

**CSS (style.css):**

```css
.search-container {
    position: relative; /* Essential for positioning the icon inside */
    display: flex; /* Helps align input and button horizontally */
    width: 300px; /* Adjust as needed */
    border: 1px solid #ccc; /* Example border for the whole container */
    border-radius: 5px;
    overflow: hidden; /* Ensures icon stays within container if it overflows slightly */
}

#searchInput {
    flex-grow: 1; /* Allows input to take up available space */
    padding: 10px;
    border: none; /* Remove default input border */
    outline: none; /* Remove outline on focus */
    font-size: 16px;
}

.search-icon-button {
    background: none;
    border: none;
    cursor: pointer;
    padding: 8px 12px; /* Adjust padding as needed */
    display: flex;
    align-items: center;
    justify-content: center;
    color: #555; /* Icon color */
    transition: color 0.2s ease-in-out;
}

.search-icon-button:hover {
    color: #007bff; /* Hover effect for the icon */
}

.search-icon {
    width: 20px; /* Adjust icon size */
    height: 20px; /* Adjust icon size */
    display: block; /* Ensures the SVG itself behaves as a block element */
}

/* Optional: Focus style for the container */
.search-container:focus-within {
    border-color: #007bff;
    box-shadow: 0 0 0 3px rgba(0, 123, 255, 0.25);
}
```

**JavaScript (script.js):**

```javascript
function handleSearch() {
    const searchTerm = document.getElementById('searchInput').value;
    if (searchTerm) {
        alert(`Searching for: "${searchTerm}"`);
        // In a real application, you would perform an actual search here
        // e.g., make an API call, filter a list, etc.
    } else {
        alert("Please enter something to search for!");
    }
}

// Optional: You can also set up an event listener for the 'Enter' key on the input
document.getElementById('searchInput').addEventListener('keypress', function(event) {
    if (event.key === 'Enter') {
        handleSearch();
    }
});
```

**Explanation:**

1.  **HTML Structure:**
    *   **`.search-container`:** This `div` acts as the parent container. `position: relative;` is crucial here because it allows us to absolutely position the icon *relative to this container*. `display: flex;` makes the input and button sit side-by-side naturally.
    *   **`input type="text"`:** Your search input field.
    *   **`button type="button" class="search-icon-button"`:** We wrap the SVG in a `button` for better accessibility and built-in click handling. Using `type="button"` prevents unintentional form submissions if this were inside a `<form>` tag.
    *   **SVG Icon:** I've included a common "reading glass" SVG icon. You can replace this with any SVG you have. The `currentColor` fill allows the icon to inherit the color from its parent (the button's text color in this case).
    *   **`onclick="handleSearch()"`:** This inline JavaScript directly calls your `handleSearch` function when the button is clicked.

2.  **CSS Styling:**
    *   **`.search-container`:**
        *   `position: relative;`: This is the key. It establishes a positioning context for any absolutely positioned children.
        *   `display: flex;`: Arranges the input and button horizontally.
        *   `border` and `border-radius`: Styles the overall appearance of the combined input and icon.
        *   `overflow: hidden;`: Ensures that if the icon were to somehow extend beyond the container's bounds, it would be clipped.
    *   **`#searchInput`:**
        *   `flex-grow: 1;`: Makes the input expand to fill the available space within the flex container.
        *   `border: none; outline: none;`: Removes the default browser styling for inputs, as the container now provides the visual border.
    *   **`.search-icon-button`:**
        *   `background: none; border: none;`: Removes default button styling so only the icon is visible.
        *   `cursor: pointer;`: Indicates that it's clickable.
        *   `padding`: Adds some space around the icon.
        *   `display: flex; align-items: center; justify-content: center;`: Helps center the SVG within the button.
        *   `color`: Sets the icon's color (which will be inherited by the `currentColor` in the SVG).
        *   `transition`: Adds a smooth color change on hover.
    *   **`.search-icon`:**
        *   `width` and `height`: Sets the actual size of your SVG icon.
        *   `display: block;`: Ensures the SVG takes up its own space correctly.
    *   **`.search-container:focus-within` (Optional):** This pseudo-class applies styles when any element *inside* the container is focused (e.g., when the input field is active). It's a nice accessibility touch.

3.  **JavaScript Functionality:**
    *   **`handleSearch()`:** This function is executed when the search icon (button) is clicked.
        *   It retrieves the current value from the `searchInput` field.
        *   It then shows an `alert` with the search term. In a real application, you'd replace this with your actual search logic (e.g., making an API request, filtering data on the page, etc.).
    *   **`keypress` event listener (Optional):** This is a common and user-friendly addition. It listens for the `Enter` key press on the input field and calls `handleSearch()` automatically, mimicking the behavior of many search forms.

**How to Use:**

1.  Save the HTML code as `index.html`.
2.  Save the CSS code as `style.css` in the same directory.
3.  Save the JavaScript code as `script.js` in the same directory.
4.  Open `index.html` in your web browser.

Now you'll have an input field with a clickable reading glass icon on its right side, and when you click it (or press Enter in the input), your `handleSearch` function will be called.

